.TH INK 1 "August 2026" "Ink" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
ink \- small terminal-based text editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ink
.RI [ FILE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ink
is a small, terminal-based text editor written in Rust.

It is designed to be lightweight and keyboard-driven, with a simple command
language and a small codebase.

If
.I FILE
is supplied, it is opened when Ink starts. If no file is supplied, Ink starts
with an empty buffer.

Ink supports multiple open buffers. Buffers may contain files from disk or
generated content such as command output and help.

.SH COMMAND MODE
Press
.B Esc
while editing to enter the command bar.

Commands consist of a command letter followed by optional arguments. Arguments
are enclosed by semicolons and multiple arguments are separated by colons.

For example:

.EX
e;file.txt;
g;42;
x;ls -la;
.EE

Commands can be modified using postfix modifiers.

.TP
.B !
Force an operation when the command supports forced execution.

.TP
.B ?
Show help for the command.

.TP
.B *N
Repeat the command
.I N
times.

.TP
.B ,
Create/Close a block, other modifiers can modify the entirety of the block.

For example:

.EX
q!
g?
g*5
.EE

A bare
.B ?
has no command before it and opens the general Ink manual inside the editor.

.SH ARGUMENTS
Command arguments are normally terminated by
.BR ;
and separated by
.BR : .

For example:

.EX
e;file.txt;
.EE

Arguments may also be written as quoted literals when the command language
requires them.

.SH MOTIONS
Ink provides motion nodes which can be used as part of the command language.

.EX
>       move up one line
>5      move up five lines
<       move down one line
<5      move down five lines
.EE

Motion directions use the editor's command-language convention and are
independent of normal cursor key input.

.SH GOTO
The goto command moves the cursor to a specified position.

A line number moves to the corresponding line:

.EX
g;42;
.EE

Ink also supports named positions:

.TP
.B sf
Start of file.

.TP
.B ef
End of file.

.TP
.B sl
Start of line.

.TP
.B el
End of line.

For example:

.EX
g;sf;
g;ef;
g;sl;
g;el;
.EE

.SH BUFFERS
Ink can have multiple documents open at the same time.

Each open document is represented by a buffer and the active buffer is shown
in the buffer bar at the top of the screen.

Opening another file creates another buffer rather than replacing the current
one.

Generated content can also be opened as a buffer. This is used by features
such as command output and help.

.SH GENERATED BUFFERS
Some commands produce content which is not associated with a file on disk.

Examples include command output and the Ink manual.

Generated buffers use the same document and terminal rendering mechanisms as
normal files and can therefore be navigated like regular buffers.

Such buffers may be read-only.

.SH FILES
When a file is opened, Ink stores its path with the document.

Modified documents are marked as dirty in the buffer bar and status bar.

Commands which would discard a modified document may require the force
modifier
.B !
to proceed.

.SH EDITING
Ink is primarily controlled through the keyboard.

The normal editing mode provides character insertion, deletion, cursor
movement, word movement, indentation, and line editing.

Common controls include:

.TP
.B Esc
Enter command mode.

.TP
.B Enter
Insert a new line.

.TP
.B Backspace
Delete backwards.

.TP
.B Delete
Delete forwards.

.TP
.B Ctrl-Left
Move one word backwards.

.TP
.B Ctrl-Right
Move one word forwards.

.TP
.B Ctrl-W
Delete the previous word.

.TP
.B Ctrl-Delete
Delete the next word.

.SH HELP
Ink provides built-in help through the command language.

Append
.B ?
to a command to request information about that command:

.EX
g?
q?
e?
.EE

Use a bare
.B ?
to open the general Ink manual:

.EX
?
.EE

The help is opened as a buffer rather than displayed only as a temporary
message.

.SH EXAMPLES
Open an existing file:

.EX
ink main.rs
.EE

Start with an empty buffer:

.EX
ink
.EE

Open another file from inside Ink:

.EX
e;other.rs;
.EE

Move to line 42:

.EX
g;42;
.EE

Move to the end of the file:

.EX
g;ef;
.EE

Run a shell command:

.EX
x;cargo check;
.EE

Display help for a command:

.EX
g?
.EE

Display the general manual:

.EX
?
.EE

.SH FLAGS

The flags that Ink can be provided are:

.TP
.B -r / --readonly
Enable read only mode for the file being opened

.SH ENVIRONMENT
Ink does not currently require any Ink-specific environment variables.

.SH FILE FORMAT
Ink edits text files and preserves their contents through the
.B Document
buffer model.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Ink exited normally.

.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors and temporary warnings are displayed through Ink's warning interface.

Command parsing errors report the position at which the parser encountered the
problem.

.SH BUGS
Ink is still under active development and is not intended to be a drop-in
replacement for mature terminal editors.

Behaviour and internal interfaces may change.

Bug reports and feature requests should be made through the project's issue
tracker.

